by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-02T13:41:09Z

Good point, and documenting it would not have been enough: the recipe we would have documented was "write a controller that calls `start()` on `security.authenticator.oidc_login.<firewall>`", and that service id is wiring, not API. So instead of documenting a workaround, I folded the missing piece into this PR (f0ac2c059): the route loader now declares a `_oidc_login_start_<firewall>` route at the new `start_path` (default `/oidc/start`), served by a small public controller that delegates to the firewall authenticator's `start()`.

The login template then needs one line, with `entry_point` pointing at the form:

```twig
<a href="{{ path('_oidc_login_start_main') }}">Sign in with the provider</a>
```

`start_path` follows the `check_path` conventions (a route name declares nothing), a start path cannot be shared between two firewalls since the route carries the firewall name, and the whole thing is covered by a functional test that drives `GET /oidc/start` against a mocked discovery and asserts the provider redirect, PKCE challenge included. Description updated. The docs will have to mention that the start path must stay anonymously accessible when `access_control` rules cover the firewall.
